  "full_review": "Dhirteen Conversations About One Thing\" (Sony Classics) is a thoughtful meditation on the elusiveness of happiness as experienced by several New Yorkers whose lives briefly intersect.\n\n���� Director Jill Sprecher co-wrote the low-budget, independent drama with her sister Karen, their second film since 1997's \"Clockwatchers.\" An intelligent if downbeat tale, the ensemble film follows a year in the life of various Manhattanites.\n\n���� Troy (Matthew McConaughey) is a cocky prosecuting attorney until he accidentally runs someone down and is consumed by guilt.\n\n���� Hard-working Gene (Alan Arkin), crushed by a divorce and his son's drug addiction, takes his misery out on his staff, especially eternally cheerful Wade (Bill Wise).\n\n���� Another optimist, young housecleaner Beatrice (Clea DuVall), has the wind taken out of her sails when she is gravely injured and left for dead.\n\n���� A morose physics professor (John Turturro) futilely seeks novelty in an extramarital affair, leaving his wife (Amy Irving) at a loss to deal with her painful loneliness.\n\n���� Each comes to question the meaning of life and his or her place in it. Although the film's tone is solemn to the point of gloomy, most of the characters eventually make tiny steps toward hope and renewal and this comes across in understated, credible ways, in gestures as simple as a wave or a smile.\n\n���� The film reminds the audience how one never realizes the profound effect a look of kindness or simple word of encouragement can have on a troubled person. And this comes directly from two incidents in the director's own life which plunged her into hateful anger until a stranger's smile stopped her in her tracks.\n\n���� Although the characters express no specific religious convictions, Troy and Gene act on their pangs of conscience while Beatrice clearly has a spiritual bent.\n\n���� Sprecher mines her characters' fragile psyches with real compassion and keeps the narrative more involving by not showing the story chronologically. The muted-color production design and location shooting reflect the isolation and solitude of Manhattan's forbidding concrete canyons.\n\n���� While all of the cast is quite good, the doleful Arkin is outstanding, as is DuVallin conveying her character's complete-circle transformation. And Frankie Faison as Gene's sympathetic co-worker sums it all up when he concludes, \"Life only makes sense when you look at in backwards; too bad we gottalive it forward.\"\n\n���� Unlike popcorn movies, \"Thirteen Conversations\" offers something to talk about afterward.\n\n���� Because of fleeting violence, an implied affair, references to suicide, some rough language and occasional profanity, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-III -- adults. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is R -- restricted."
}